A vibrant, elegant side dish that transforms a humble bag of baby carrots into a showpiece with just a handful of ingredients. Simmering baby carrots directly in melted butter, honey, and brown sugar caramelizes their natural sugars, while fresh dill and thyme bring an earthy, herbal balance to the sweetness.

*Adapted from [Damn Delicious](https://damndelicious.net/2013/11/25/honey-glazed-baby-carrots/).*

## Ingredients

- 2 tablespoons unsalted butter
- 1 lb (16 oz) baby carrots (rinsed and patted thoroughly dry)
- 2 tablespoons pure honey
- 2 tablespoons packed light brown sugar
- 2 teaspoons fresh dill, finely minced
- 2 teaspoons fresh thyme leaves, stripped from stems
- ¼ teaspoon kosher salt (or to taste)
- ⅛ teaspoon freshly cracked black pepper

## Instructions

1. **Melt the Butter Base:** Place a large 10-to-12-inch wide skillet over medium heat. Add the unsalted butter and let it melt until bubbly.
2. **Add Carrots, Sweeteners, and Herbs:** Dump the baby carrots into the skillet. Drizzle the honey over the carrots, sprinkle with the packed brown sugar, and add the freshly minced dill, thyme leaves, kosher salt, and cracked black pepper.
3. **Coat the Carrots:** Use a wooden spoon or heatproof spatula to toss everything together. The brown sugar and honey will melt into the butter, creating an aromatic, herb-flecked sauce that coats all the carrots.
4. **Glaze and Cook to Fork-Tender:** Cook uncovered over medium heat for 12 to 15 minutes, stirring and tossing every 2 to 3 minutes so the glaze doesn't burn. As moisture evaporates, the sauce will thicken into a rich, glossy glaze that clings to the carrots, and the carrots will soften until easily pierced with a fork with a delicate bite.
5. **Plate and Serve:** Remove the skillet from heat. Transfer the glazed carrots to a shallow serving platter, spooning all the warm butter-herb syrup from the pan across the top. Garnish with a tiny pinch of additional fresh thyme or dill and serve immediately.

## Notes

- **Whole Carrot Option:** Slender whole Dutch or rainbow carrots (peeled with tops trimmed to ½ inch) work equally well; simply increase cooking time by 3 to 5 minutes or cover the skillet for the first 5 minutes to help them steam.
- **Herbal Variations:** If fresh dill isn't on hand, replace with chopped fresh flat-leaf parsley or finely minced fresh rosemary.
- **Pairing Ideas:** A natural partner for roasted poultry, grilled pork chops, baked ham, or pot roast.
